    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine. The M 282.814 is the facelifted, 48V mild-hybrid iteration of the 1.33L block. It retains the highly robust, silent-type timing chain system of the pre-facelift models. Catastrophic chain stretching is incredibly rare. However, the hydraulic timing chain tensioner still relies entirely on pristine oil pressure. Extending oil changes beyond 10,000 km will cause sludge to block the tensioner's micro-galleries, leading to chain slack and a distinct rattling noise. The most significant mechanical change in this area is the addition of the RSG (Belt-Driven Starter Generator) which places massive tension and torque loads on the auxiliary serpentine belt. The auxiliary belt and its specialized tensioner must be inspected constantly, as a snapped belt will disable the entire 48V hybrid system and immobilize the car.)",
    injectionType: "Direct Injection (Features the same advanced Bosch high-pressure direct injection system operating at 250 bar. The injectors are centrally mounted in the 'delta' cylinder head. While this provides phenomenal fuel efficiency, it still guarantees severe intake valve carbon buildup because the fuel bypasses the intake valves entirely. A professional Walnut Blasting service is absolutely required every 80,000 to 100,000 kilometers to restore airflow and prevent misfires. The high-pressure fuel pump (HPFP) can occasionally suffer from internal seal leaks, diluting engine oil with raw petrol.)",
    engineLifespan: "200,000 - 250,000+ km (The mechanical core of the M 282 remains an ultra-lightweight aluminum block with bore spray coating, making it highly durable if properly lubricated. The addition of the 48V Mild Hybrid system, however, introduces a massive layer of electronic complexity that threatens the car's long-term economic lifespan. While the engine block will easily exceed 250,000 km, the highly expensive 48V lithium-ion battery and the RSG (Starter Generator) are known wear items. When the 48V battery inevitably degrades and fails out of warranty, the astronomical replacement cost frequently totals the vehicle economically.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Intercooled. Equipped with a highly responsive wastegate turbocharger. In this MHEV variant, the turbocharger is significantly aided by the 48V RSG unit, which instantly fills in the low-RPM torque gap before the turbo spools. This 'EQ Boost' makes the engine feel much larger and more responsive than it is. The mechanical linkage rod connecting the electronic wastegate actuator to the wastegate flap still wears out over time due to heat cycling, causing a metallic rattling sound during deceleration. If the flap fails to seal properly, it triggers Limp Mode.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch MED 17 / Hybrid Controller (A highly complex, integrated engine and hybrid management system. It meticulously oversees the direct injection, electronic boost control, and intricately balances the 14 hp / 150 Nm electrical boost provided by the 48V RSG unit. The ECU logic allows for seamless 'gliding' with the combustion engine completely shut off at highway speeds. Tuning this MHEV variant is significantly more complex than the pre-facelift models due to the delicate software handshake required between the combustion ECU and the 48V battery controller. Improper tuning can cause severe hybrid system faults.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 6d (Equipped with GPF - Gasoline Particulate Filter)",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "170 - 180 hp (The internal rotating assembly handles power effortlessly, as it shares architecture with the 163hp A 200 MHEV. A Stage 1 tune can safely extract more power from the combustion engine, but tuners must be extremely careful not to disrupt the 48V EQ Boost logic. Pushing beyond 180hp places immense strain on the Getrag 7DCT300 transmission's internal wet clutch packs, which are already managing the instant, harsh electrical torque from the RSG unit.)",
