    timingType: "Chain (Located at the REAR of the engine block. The M 264 utilizes a highly complex simplex timing chain system situated between the engine and the transmission. This is a massive departure from the front-mounted chain of the older M270. While catastrophic stretching is less common, the rear placement makes any timing service—such as replacing a noisy tensioner or worn chain guides—an absolute nightmare. Accessing the timing components requires physically dropping the entire engine and front subframe out of the vehicle. This turns preventative maintenance into an astronomically expensive, multi-day labor job.)",
    injectionType: "Direct Injection (Features a highly advanced Bosch high-pressure direct injection system operating at 250 bar. The Piezo injectors are centrally mounted to optimize the spray pattern inside the combustion chamber. This provides phenomenal fuel atomization but guarantees severe intake valve carbon buildup because the fuel entirely bypasses the intake valves. A professional Walnut Blasting service is absolutely required every 80,000 to 100,000 kilometers to restore factory airflow, cure rough cold idling, and prevent dangerous cylinder misfires. The high-pressure fuel pump can occasionally suffer from internal seal leaks, diluting engine oil with raw petrol.)",
    engineLifespan: "250,000+ km (The mechanical core of the M 264 is an exceptionally robust, ultra-modern die-cast aluminum block featuring Nanoslide bore spray coating and CONICSHAPE cylinder honing to drastically reduce internal friction. Because the A 220 is heavily software-restricted to 190 horsepower, the internal rotating assembly is virtually unstressed and will easily outlive the chassis if properly lubricated. The vehicle's actual lifespan is dictated entirely by its auxiliary components: the rear timing chain, the complex thermal management module, and the 7G-DCT transmission.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Intercooled. Equipped with a highly responsive twin-scroll turbocharger featuring an electronic wastegate actuator. The twin-scroll design perfectly separates the exhaust pulses, drastically reducing turbo lag and providing immediate throttle response. Because the A 220 is detuned from the 224hp A 250, the turbocharger operates well below its thermal limits. However, the mechanical linkage rod connecting the electronic actuator to the wastegate flap wears out over time due to intense heat cycling. This causes a distinct metallic rattling sound during deceleration and cold starts.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch MED 17.7.7 (A highly complex, standalone engine management system overseeing direct injection, electronic boost control, CAMTRONIC variable valve lift, and the thermal management module. The 2.0-liter M 264 physical hardware is absolutely identical across the A 220 (190hp) and A 250 (224hp). The 190hp output is strictly an artificial software limitation. Consequently, the ECU is incredibly receptive to OBD2 flashing, allowing tuners to instantly and safely unlock a massive 50+ horsepower without changing a single mechanical part, safely bypassing the factory restriction.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 6d-TEMP / Local Spec (Often without GPF depending on the market)",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "240 - 260 hp (Because the engine block, turbo, and injectors are physically identical to the 224hp A 250, the stock internals handle 250 horsepower effortlessly. Removing the factory software restriction via a Stage 1 tune is the most cost-effective modification, completely transforming the A 220 into a genuinely fast hot hatch. Pushing beyond 260hp requires a larger turbocharger, upgraded intercooler, and a high-flow downpipe, at which point the 7G-DCT transmission's internal wet clutch packs become the primary, fragile limiting factor.)",
