    commonIssues: [
      "Thermal Management Module Failure: The M 260 abandons traditional thermostats for a highly complex, electronically controlled rotary valve module that directs coolant. The internal plastic gears wear out or the electric motor burns out, causing the valve to jam. This leads to severe overheating or the engine running constantly cold, requiring an expensive module replacement.",
      "Rear Timing Chain Rattle: The timing chain is located at the back of the engine. The hydraulic tensioner frequently degrades, causing the chain to slap against the plastic guides on cold starts. Ignoring this rattle risks the chain jumping teeth, and repairing it requires dropping the entire engine out of the car.",
      "7G-DCT Mechatronic & Solenoid Contamination: The 350 Nm of torque generates significant friction material from the wet clutch packs. If the transmission fluid and filters are not changed strictly every 40,000 miles, the metallic debris clogs the microscopic passages in the Mechatronic unit. This results in harsh, jerky gear engagements and eventual total transmission failure.",
      "Intake Valve Carbon Buildup: Due to the high-pressure direct injection entirely bypassing the intake valves, PCV oil vapor bakes into a thick, sticky carbon sludge on the hot valve stems. This heavily restricts airflow, causing rough cold idles, severe hesitation, and dangerous high-RPM misfires until professionally Walnut Blasted.",
      "Electronic Wastegate Rattle: The mechanical linkage rod connecting the electronic actuator to the twin-scroll turbocharger wastegate flap wears out prematurely from extreme heat cycling. This causes a distinct, loud metallic rattle during deceleration. If the flap fails to close completely, it triggers underboost codes and Limp Mode.",
      "Fuel Injector / HPFP Failure (USA Fuel Quality): In the US market with varying fuel qualities or higher ethanol blends, the highly sensitive Bosch direct injectors and the High-Pressure Fuel Pump can suffer from internal scoring and seal degradation, leading to misfires and raw fuel leaking into the oil.",
      "CAMTRONIC Actuator Failure: The engine features variable valve lift on the intake camshaft. The electronic actuators controlling the pin adjustment can fail, causing the engine to stay locked in the low-lift profile. This results in a sudden and massive loss of high-RPM power and illuminates the Check Engine Light."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Economic Total Loss (Rear Chain Labor): Because the timing chain is wedged against the firewall, fixing a worn chain or shattered guides requires physically unbolting and dropping the entire engine and front subframe out of the vehicle. This turns a routine repair into a massive, multi-day labor nightmare costing thousands of dollars.",
      "Economic Total Loss (DCT Mechatronic Failure): Neglecting the 7G-DCT fluid changes will relentlessly destroy the internal wet clutch pack and the highly sensitive Mechatronic control unit. A full transmission rebuild easily costs upwards of $3,000 USD, heavily threatening the car's economic viability.",
      "Cylinder Head Warping (Thermal Module Jam): If the complex rotary thermal management module jams closed, the engine will dump its coolant or block flow to the radiator. The ultra-modern, lightweight aluminum block and cylinder head will warp and crack incredibly fast if the driver attempts to keep driving while overheating.",
      "Drivability Loss (Carbon Choking): Severe carbon and oil sludge buildup on the intake valves will eventually restrict airflow so heavily that the engine physically cannot breathe at higher RPMs. The engine will suffer from random cylinder misfires, incredibly poor acceleration, and will force the ECU into Limp Mode.",
      "Fuel Dilution (HPFP Leak): The high-pressure fuel pump (HPFP) can occasionally suffer a failure of its internal shaft seal, exacerbated by poor fuel quality. This leaks highly pressurized, raw fuel directly into the engine's oil supply. This completely ruins the oil's lubricity and can quickly wipe out the main engine bearings if the oil level inexplicably rises.",
      "Ignition Coil Melt (Ignored Spark Plugs): If the delicate spark plugs are left in past their 40,000-mile service life, the increasing electrical resistance forces the ignition coils to work significantly harder to jump the gap. This eventually causes the coils to overheat, melt internally, and leave the driver stranded."
    ],
