    yearRange: "2019-present",
    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine. The A 250 e utilizes the 1.33L M 282 engine jointly developed with Renault/Nissan. It features a highly robust, silent-type timing chain system. Catastrophic chain stretching is incredibly rare on this architecture. However, the hydraulic timing chain tensioner relies entirely on pristine oil pressure. In a PHEV, the combustion engine is frequently shut off and then suddenly demanded to provide full power while still cold. This intense thermal shock requires absolute adherence to the 10,000 km oil change intervals to prevent sludge buildup in the tensioner.)",
    injectionType: "Direct Injection (Features a highly advanced Bosch high-pressure direct injection system operating at 250 bar. While this provides phenomenal fuel efficiency, it guarantees severe intake valve carbon buildup because the fuel entirely bypasses the intake valves. This issue is heavily exacerbated in the PHEV because the engine rarely runs long enough on short trips to reach optimal self-cleaning temperatures. A professional Walnut Blasting service is absolutely required every 80,000 to 100,000 kilometers to restore factory airflow and prevent dangerous cylinder misfires under load.)",
    engineLifespan: "200,000 - 250,000+ km (The mechanical core of the M 282 is an ultra-lightweight aluminum block featuring a unique delta-shaped cylinder head. The combustion engine itself is virtually unstressed and reliable. The vehicle's actual lifespan is dictated entirely by its incredibly complex high-voltage hybrid components. The 15.6 kWh lithium-ion battery pack and the integrated 75kW electric motor inside the 8G-DCT transmission are massive long-term financial liabilities. When the high-voltage battery inevitably degrades beyond acceptable range or fails out of warranty, the astronomical replacement cost frequently totals the vehicle economically.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Intercooled. Equipped with a highly responsive, low-inertia wastegate turbocharger. The turbocharger operates seamlessly with the 75kW electric motor to provide massive combined torque. However, because the engine is often suddenly activated at high speeds when the battery depletes, the turbocharger undergoes extreme thermal shock. The mechanical linkage rod connecting the electronic wastegate actuator to the wastegate flap wears out over time due to this intense heat cycling, causing a highly annoying metallic rattling sound during deceleration.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch MED 17 / Hybrid Powertrain Control Unit (A highly complex, dual-brain management system that meticulously oversees the direct injection, the electronic boost control, and intricately manages the handoff between the 160hp combustion engine and the 75kW electric motor. The hybrid logic is designed to maximize electric range and regenerate energy smoothly. Tuning a PHEV variant is highly complex and generally discouraged; improper tuning can severely disrupt the hybrid handshake, leading to violent transmission engagements or destructive electrical surges.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 6d-TEMP / Euro 6d (Equipped with GPF)",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "230 - 240 hp (The internal rotating assembly of the 1.33L block handles its standalone 160hp share effortlessly. While the combustion engine can be tuned, the absolute limiting factor of the A 250 e is the specialized 8G-DCT (8F-DCT) transmission. It is already tasked with managing a massive 450 Nm of combined torque. Pushing more power from the combustion engine will rapidly overwhelm the transmission's wet clutch packs, which are integrated directly with the electric motor, leading to catastrophic and immensely expensive drivetrain failure.)",
