    torque: "400 Nm",
    fuelType: "Petrol Mild Hybrid",
    cylinders: 4,
    valves: 16,
    compression: "10.0:1",
    yearRange: "2023-present",
    timingType: "Chain (Located at the REAR of the engine block. The M 260.820 is the 48V mild-hybrid iteration of the 2.0L AMG block. It retains the highly complex simplex timing chain system situated between the engine and the transmission. This rear placement makes any timing service an absolute nightmare, requiring the entire engine and front subframe to be dropped. The most significant mechanical change here is the addition of the RSG (Belt-Driven Starter Generator) which places massive tension and torque loads on the auxiliary serpentine belt. A snapped belt will disable the entire 48V hybrid system and immobilize the car.)",
    injectionType: "Direct Injection (Features a highly advanced Bosch high-pressure direct injection system operating at 250 bar. The Piezo injectors optimize spray, providing phenomenal fuel atomization but guaranteeing severe intake valve carbon buildup. Professional Walnut Blasting is required every 60,000 to 80,000 kilometers to restore airflow and prevent misfires. The High-Pressure Fuel Pump must flow immense volumes of fuel to support the massive boost pressure.)",
    engineLifespan: "150,000 - 250,000+ km (The mechanical core is an exceptionally robust die-cast aluminum block. By the time this facelift launched, Mercedes had thankfully resolved the catastrophic cylinder head exhaust valve seat recession issues that plagued the early models. However, the addition of the 48V Mild Hybrid system introduces a massive layer of electronic complexity. When the highly expensive 48V lithium-ion battery inevitably degrades and fails out of warranty, the astronomical replacement cost frequently totals the vehicle economically.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Intercooled. Equipped with a massive twin-scroll turbocharger. In this MHEV variant, the turbocharger is significantly aided by the 48V RSG unit, which instantly fills in the low-RPM torque gap before the massive turbo spools. This 'EQ Boost' provides immediate, massive low-end torque. The mechanical linkage rod connecting the electronic wastegate actuator to the wastegate flap still wears out over time due to intense heat cycling, causing a metallic rattling sound during deceleration.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch MED 17.7.7 / Hybrid Controller (A highly complex, integrated engine and hybrid management system. It meticulously oversees the direct injection, twin-scroll boost control, and intricately balances the 14 hp / 150 Nm electrical boost provided by the 48V RSG unit. The ECU is aggressively mapped for AMG performance. Tuning this MHEV variant is significantly more complex due to the delicate software handshake required between the combustion ECU and the 48V battery controller.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 6d (Equipped with GPF - Gasoline Particulate Filter)",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "360 - 380 hp (The internal rotating assembly handles power effortlessly. A Stage 1 tune can safely extract more power from the combustion engine, but tuners must be extremely careful not to disrupt the 48V EQ Boost logic. Pushing beyond 380hp places immense strain on the newly introduced 8-speed AMG Speedshift transmission's internal wet clutch packs, which are already managing the instant electrical torque.)",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "9.5 - 11.5 L/100km",
      highway: "6.5 - 8.0 L/100km",
      mixed: "8.0 - 9.5 L/100km",
      sporty: "13.0+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "0W-20 or 5W-30 Synthetic (MB 229.71 strictly required for GPF protection and MHEV efficiency)",
    commonIssues: [
      "48V Battery Degradation & Failure: The absolute most terrifying and expensive flaw of the MHEV models. The 48V lithium-ion battery located under the floor degrades over time and from extreme temperature cycling. When it fails, the entire hybrid system shuts down, the car refuses to start, and a massive red warning appears on the dash. Replacement is astronomically expensive.",
      "RSG Belt Tensioner Wear: The Belt-Driven Starter Generator (RSG) applies a massive 150 Nm of electrical torque directly through the auxiliary serpentine belt to start the engine instantly and provide boost. This extreme stress causes the specialized belt tensioner and the belt itself to wear out rapidly, requiring premature replacement to prevent snapping.",
      "Thermal Management Module Failure: The M 260 abandons traditional thermostats for an electronically controlled rotary valve module. The internal plastic gears wear out, causing the valve to jam. This leads to severe overheating or the engine running constantly cold, requiring an expensive module replacement.",
      "Rear Timing Chain Rattle: The timing chain is located at the absolute back of the engine. The hydraulic tensioner frequently degrades, causing the chain to slap against the plastic guides on cold starts. Ignoring this rattle risks the chain jumping teeth. Repairing it requires dropping the entire engine out of the car.",
      "8G-DCT Mechatronic Contamination: The new 8-speed wet dual-clutch transmission is heavily stressed by the instant electrical torque from the RSG combined with the combustion engine's 400Nm. If the fluid and filters are not changed strictly every 40,000 km, the clutch packs slip and the Mechatronic solenoids clog.",
      "Intake Valve Carbon Buildup: Due to the high-pressure direct injection entirely bypassing the intake valves, PCV oil vapor bakes into a thick, sticky carbon sludge on the hot valve stems. This heavily restricts airflow, requiring professional Walnut Blasting every 60k-80k km.",
      "Electronic Wastegate Rattle: The mechanical linkage rod connecting the electronic actuator to the massive twin-scroll turbocharger wastegate flap wears out prematurely from extreme heat cycling. This causes a distinct, loud metallic rattle during deceleration."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Economic Total Loss (48V System Failure): When the 48V lithium-ion battery or the RSG unit fails out of warranty, the repair bills routinely exceed 3,000 EUR. Because the car cannot function without the 48V system, this single failure threatens the entire economic viability of the vehicle as it depreciates. It is the absolute biggest risk of buying a used MHEV.",
      "Economic Total Loss (Rear Chain Labor): Because the timing chain is wedged against the firewall, fixing a worn chain or shattered guides requires physically unbolting and dropping the entire engine and front subframe out of the vehicle. This turns a routine repair into a massive, multi-day labor nightmare costing thousands of euros.",
      "Immobilization (Auxiliary Belt Snap): Because there is no traditional 12V starter motor, the car relies entirely on the RSG turning the serpentine belt to start the engine. If the heavily stressed serpentine belt snaps due to tensioner failure, the car is instantly immobilized and cannot even be jump-started.",
      "Cylinder Head Warping (Thermal Module Jam): If the complex rotary thermal management module jams closed, the engine will dump its coolant or block flow to the radiator. The ultra-modern, lightweight aluminum block and cylinder head will warp and crack incredibly fast if the driver attempts to keep driving while overheating.",
      "Economic Total Loss (DCT Mechatronic Failure): Neglecting the AMG 8G-DCT fluid changes will relentlessly destroy the internal wet clutch pack and the highly sensitive Mechatronic control unit. A full transmission rebuild costs upwards of 3,500 EUR. This is especially critical given the extra instant torque from the RSG and Launch Control abuse.",
      "Drivability Loss (Carbon Choking): Severe carbon and oil sludge buildup on the intake valves will eventually restrict airflow so heavily that the engine physically cannot breathe at higher RPMs. The engine will suffer from random cylinder misfires, incredibly poor acceleration, and will force the ECU into Limp Mode."
    ],
