    cylinders: 4,
    valves: 16,
    compression: "9.0:1",
    yearRange: "2019-present",
    timingType: "Chain (Located at the REAR of the engine block. The M 139 utilizes a massively upgraded, heavy-duty timing chain system compared to the M260, designed to withstand the immense 480 Nm of torque and 7200 RPM redline. While catastrophic chain snapping is exceptionally rare, the hydraulic tensioners are still subjected to extreme thermal loads and can degrade. Crucially, the rear-mounted location means that any timing-related service dictates that the entire engine and transmission assembly must be physically removed from the vehicle. This turns any timing repair into an astronomical, multi-day labor nightmare.)",
    injectionType: "Dual Stage Injection (Direct + Port Injection). (This is the defining reliability upgrade of the M139 over standard Mercedes engines. It features a highly sophisticated two-stage system: high-pressure Piezo injectors (200 bar) spray directly into the cylinders, while secondary solenoid injectors spray into the intake manifold. This dual-injection setup brillianty serves a dual purpose: it fuels the massive 387hp output, and the port injectors continuously wash the intake valves with raw fuel. This completely eliminates the severe carbon choking issues that plague standard direct-injected Mercedes engines. No Walnut Blasting is required.)",
    engineLifespan: "150,000 - 250,000+ km (The M 139 is an entirely hand-built masterpiece from Affalterbach, following the 'One Man, One Engine' philosophy. The closed-deck aluminum block features chill-cast construction and forged internals, making it structurally capable of withstanding immense boost pressures. However, because it produces nearly 200hp per liter, it operates at the absolute physical limits of thermal dynamics. The lifespan is entirely dependent on meticulous, obsessive maintenance. The extreme ambient heat in the engine bay rapidly bakes all plastic cooling lines and wiring harnesses, causing them to become brittle and fail.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Intercooled. Equipped with a colossal, highly specialized twin-scroll turbocharger featuring roller bearings for insanely fast spooling. Mercedes drastically re-engineered the M139 by rotating the engine 180 degrees; the intake is now at the front, and the massive turbocharger sits at the rear firewall. This optimizes aerodynamics and shortens intake tracts but completely cooks the firewall area. The turbo runs an astonishing 1.9 bar (27.5 psi) of boost. It is cooled by a dedicated electric water pump that continues running after the engine is shut off to prevent the oil from coking inside the ultra-expensive roller bearings.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch MED 17.7.8 (A highly sophisticated, bespoke AMG engine management system. It meticulously controls the massive boost pressure, the dual-stage injection transition, and interfaces perfectly with the 8-speed AMG Speedshift DCT to execute violent, lightning-fast gear changes and aggressive rev-matching. Despite already producing 387hp, the M 139 is incredibly receptive to aftermarket tuning. However, tuning an engine already operating at this level of thermal stress requires extreme caution and absolute adherence to high-octane premium fuel to prevent catastrophic detonation.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 6d (Equipped with GPF - Gasoline Particulate Filter)",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "450 - 480 hp (The M 139 features a closed-deck aluminum block, forged crankshaft, and forged pistons directly from the factory. The internals are phenomenally strong. A Stage 1 tune easily pushes the engine past 430 horsepower. Pushing beyond 450hp requires an upgraded downpipe and a high-performance intercooler. The ultimate limiting factor is not the engine block, but rather the immense heat soak, the flow limit of the factory turbocharger, and the physical torque limits of the 8-speed AMG DCT clutches during Launch Control.)",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "12.0 - 15.0 L/100km",
      highway: "7.5 - 9.5 L/100km",
      mixed: "9.5 - 11.0 L/100km",
      sporty: "18.0+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "0W-20 or 5W-40 Synthetic (MB 229.71 strictly required for GPF protection and extreme heat. ONLY Premium Brands.)",
    commonIssues: [
      "Extreme Engine Bay Heat & Plastic Embrittlement: The massive turbocharger and exhaust manifold sit directly against the firewall. This completely cooks the engine bay. Every single plastic coolant flange, vent line, and wiring harness becomes incredibly brittle and snaps after a few years, causing sudden, catastrophic coolant dumps.",
      "AMG Speedshift 8G-DCT Clutch Wear: The highly stressed 8-speed dual-clutch transmission is incredibly fast but notorious for harsh, jerky shifts at low speeds. Frequent use of Launch Control (Race Start) rapidly burns out the internal wet clutch packs, requiring a highly expensive rebuild.",
      "4MATIC+ Rear Differential with Torque Control: The highly complex rear differential features twin electronically controlled multi-plate clutches to enable 'Drift Mode'. It is subjected to immense stress during AWD launches and drifting. Neglecting specific fluid changes causes the clutches to burn and the gears to whine.",
      "Spark Plug & Ignition Coil Failure: Running extreme boost pressures destroys spark plugs rapidly. Plugs MUST be changed strictly every 30,000 km to prevent spark blowout and dangerous high-RPM misfires. The extreme heat also prematurely kills ignition coils.",
      "Electric Water Pump Failure: The dedicated electric water pump responsible for cooling the turbocharger after shut-off can fail or short-circuit, draining the battery or allowing the turbo bearings to overheat and seize."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Catastrophic Engine Failure (Detonation/Misfire): Pushing a 2.0L engine to 387+ hp requires absolute perfection from the fuel and ignition systems. A failing spark plug, a clogged direct injector, or bad fuel under full 27.5 psi boost will cause instant detonation, melting a forged piston or cracking the block.",
      "Drivetrain Destruction (Launch Control Abuse): The A 45's party trick is its violent Launch Control and Drift Mode. Repeated back-to-back launches will overheat and permanently destroy the 8G-DCT clutch packs and the complex 4MATIC+ rear differential.",
      "Coolant Loss / Cylinder Head Warping: The heavy reliance on plastic for critical cooling components makes the M 139 highly vulnerable to sudden coolant leaks. Ignoring a low coolant warning will instantly warp the expensive, hand-built AMG cylinder head.",
      "Economic Loss (Turbo/Transmission): The cost of replacing the bespoke rear-mounted turbocharger or rebuilding the 8-speed AMG Speedshift DCT routinely exceeds 5,000 EUR. If a used A 45 was abused by a previous owner, it represents a massive financial disaster.",
      "Economic Total Loss (Rear Chain Labor): If the heavy-duty rear timing chain ever requires service or tensioner replacement, the entire engine and subframe must be dropped. This turns a standard timing job into a multi-thousand-euro labor nightmare."
    ],
