    timingType: "Belt (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. The OM 608 is a heavily modernized evolution of the Renault K9K architecture, retaining the traditional toothed timing belt. Unlike the complex timing chains of Mercedes' own diesels, this front-mounted belt makes serviceability incredibly straightforward and drastically reduces labor costs. However, the factory replacement interval of 10 years or 200,000 km is highly optimistic and dangerous. Mercedes specialists strictly recommend replacing the timing belt, the tensioner pulleys, and the mechanically driven water pump every 5 years or 100,000 km to completely eliminate the risk of a snapped belt causing catastrophic engine destruction.)",
    injectionType: "Common Rail Direct Injection (Features a highly advanced Continental/Delphi common rail system operating at 2000 bar. This system completely avoids the 'Black Death' injector seal failures of older engines. The solenoid injectors are highly durable but extremely sensitive to fuel quality and water contamination. The high-pressure fuel pump relies entirely on the lubricity of the diesel fuel; using low-quality diesel can cause internal pump scoring. The OM608 also integrates a highly complex Euro 6d-TEMP AdBlue (SCR) system directly into the exhaust to neutralize NOx emissions, introducing massive new reliability vulnerabilities.)",
    engineLifespan: "350,000 - 450,000+ km (The mechanical core of the OM 608 is a highly refined cast-iron block that is practically indestructible if properly lubricated. Because it only produces 116 horsepower, the internal rotating assembly is operating efficiently within its design limits. While Mercedes upgraded the connecting rod bearings from the older OM607 generation, adhering to the absurd 30,000 km factory oil change intervals will still cause them to degrade prematurely. If the oil is changed strictly every 10,000 km, the block will outlast the vehicle's electronics. The true threat to the vehicle's lifespan is the fragile AdBlue system and urban DPF clogging.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Intercooled via a water-cooled manifold integrated into the cylinder head. Equipped with a Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT) turbocharger manufactured by BorgWarner. The VNT turbocharger operates seamlessly to provide 260 Nm of torque early in the rev range. Catastrophic turbo failure is exceptionally rare if oil is kept clean. However, the vacuum actuator controlling the variable vanes can develop leaks or stick due to heavy carbon buildup from low-speed urban driving. This causes the turbo to fail to build boost, resulting in a severely lethargic Limp Mode.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch MD1 (A highly robust standalone engine management system. It meticulously controls the common rail pressure, the VNT actuator, the EGR cycle, and the highly complex AdBlue injection mapping. The ECU is mapped directly from the factory for 116hp, utilizing the hardware's native potential. It is incredibly receptive to OBD2 flashing, allowing tuners to safely unlock hidden factory power reserves and optimize the torque curve, taking the engine past 135hp.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 6d-TEMP (AdBlue / SCR)",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "135 - 145 hp (The stock internal rotating assembly handles 145 horsepower effortlessly. A Stage 1 software tune safely optimizes the VNT boost mapping and fuel delivery, making the A 180 d a significantly punchier highway cruiser. Pushing beyond 145hp on the stock VNT turbocharger will cause Exhaust Gas Temperatures (EGTs) to spike dangerously, potentially melting the exhaust valves or prematurely clogging the sensitive Euro 6 DPF/SCR matrix with excessive soot.)",
