    timingType: "Chain (Located at the REAR of the engine block. The OM 654 utilizes a complex timing chain system situated between the engine and the transmission. This rear placement makes any timing service an absolute nightmare. While catastrophic chain snapping is less common than on the older OM651, the chain guides and tensioner still wear out. Accessing the timing components requires physically dropping the entire engine and front subframe out of the vehicle, turning preventative maintenance into a massive, multi-day labor job costing thousands of euros.)",
    injectionType: "Common Rail Direct Injection (Features a highly advanced Bosch common rail system operating at an immense 2050 bar. The solenoid injectors optimize spray into the unique stepped combustion bowls. This provides phenomenal fuel atomization but generates massive soot if driven slowly. The high-pressure fuel pump (HPFP) relies entirely on the lubricity of the diesel fuel; using low-quality diesel can cause internal pump scoring, sending microscopic metal shavings through the entire fuel system. The engine integrates a highly complex Euro 6d AdBlue (SCR) system directly into the exhaust.)",
    engineLifespan: "400,000+ km (The mechanical core of the OM 654 is an exceptionally robust, ultra-modern all-aluminum block featuring Nanoslide bore spray coating and steel pistons to drastically reduce internal friction and handle massive boost. Because the A 180 d is heavily software-restricted to just 116 horsepower, the internal rotating assembly is virtually unstressed and will easily outlive the chassis if properly lubricated. The vehicle's actual lifespan is dictated entirely by its auxiliary components: the rear timing chain, the complex vacuum-actuated water pump, and the incredibly fragile AdBlue emissions system.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Intercooled. Equipped with a highly responsive Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT) turbocharger. The VNT adjusts its internal vanes to spool incredibly fast at low RPMs, providing immediate throttle response. Because the A 180 d is detuned from the 190hp A 220 d, the turbocharger operates well below its thermal limits. However, the complex variable vanes are highly prone to sticking if the car is driven exclusively in city traffic without building high EGTs to burn off soot. When the vanes stick, the car suffers from severe underboost limp modes.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch MD1 (A highly complex engine management system overseeing direct injection, VNT actuation, and the highly sensitive Euro 6 AdBlue regeneration logic. The 2.0-liter OM 654 physical hardware is absolutely identical across the 116hp A 180 d, 150hp A 200 d, and 190hp A 220 d. The 116hp output is strictly an artificial software limitation. Consequently, the ECU is incredibly receptive to OBD2 flashing, allowing tuners to instantly and safely unlock a massive 70+ horsepower without changing a single mechanical part, safely bypassing the factory restriction.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 6d (AdBlue / SCR)",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "190 - 210 hp (Because the engine block, turbo, and injectors are physically identical to the 190hp A 220 d, the stock internals handle 200 horsepower effortlessly. Removing the factory software restriction via a Stage 1 tune is the most cost-effective modification, completely transforming the A 180 d into a genuinely fast highway cruiser. Pushing beyond 210hp requires hardware upgrades, at which point the new 8G-DCT transmission's internal wet clutch packs become the primary, fragile limiting factor.)",
    fuelConsumption: {
      city: "5.5 - 6.5 L/100km",
      highway: "4.0 - 5.0 L/100km",
      mixed: "4.8 - 5.5 L/100km",
      sporty: "7.0+ L/100km"
    },
    oilSpecification: "0W-20 or 5W-30 Synthetic (MB 229.52 or 229.71 strictly required for DPF/SCR systems)",
    commonIssues: [
      "Rear-Mounted Timing Chain Stretch & Rattle: The timing chain is located at the back of the engine. The hydraulic tensioner frequently degrades, causing the chain to slap against the plastic guides on cold starts. Ignoring this rattle risks the chain jumping teeth, and repairing it requires dropping the entire engine out of the car.",
      "AdBlue (SCR) System Failure: The absolute most notorious and expensive flaw introduced with Euro 6d. The AdBlue pump, the fragile in-tank heater element, and the exhaust NOx sensors fail with alarming frequency. When any of these components fail, the dashboard initiates an un-cancellable countdown, after which the ECU completely prevents the engine from starting.",
      "Vacuum-Actuated Water Pump Leaks: The OM654 utilizes a highly complex water pump that is controlled via engine vacuum. The internal seals frequently fail, allowing engine coolant to be sucked directly into the vacuum lines. This destroys the vacuum valve block, disables the turbocharger actuator, and causes severe overheating.",
      "8G-DCT Mechatronic & Solenoid Contamination: Mercedes paired this 2.0L engine with a new 8-speed dual-clutch transmission. If the transmission fluid and filters are not changed strictly every 60,000 km, metallic debris clogs the microscopic passages in the Mechatronic unit. This results in harsh, jerky gear engagements and eventual total transmission failure.",
      "DPF Pressure Sensor & Clogging Issues: The Diesel Particulate Filter (DPF) is highly sensitive to the driving profile. Short urban trips prevent the exhaust from reaching the 600°C required for passive regeneration, causing the soot to turn into permanent ash. The differential pressure sensors frequently fail, forcing the car into a restricted 'Drivetrain Malfunction' mode.",
      "Oil Pump Wear: The variable displacement oil pump can suffer from premature wear on its internal control rings if oil changes are neglected, leading to low oil pressure warnings and potentially catastrophic bearing failure."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [
      "Economic Total Loss (Rear Chain Labor): Because the timing chain is wedged against the firewall, fixing a worn chain or shattered guides requires physically unbolting and dropping the entire engine and front subframe out of the vehicle. This turns a routine repair into a massive, multi-day labor nightmare costing thousands of euros.",
      "Immobilization (AdBlue Countdown): If any component of the highly complex Euro 6 SCR system fails, the car will literally refuse to start once the 800km safety countdown reaches zero. This will strand the driver entirely, regardless of whether the engine itself is perfectly healthy.",
      "Turbocharger Disable (Coolant in Vacuum System): If the complex vacuum water pump leaks coolant into the vacuum system, it immediately destroys the vacuum transducers. Because the turbocharger relies on this vacuum to operate the VNT vanes, the car instantly loses all boost pressure and drops into a severe Limp Mode.",
      "Economic Total Loss (8G-DCT Failure): Neglecting the 8-speed dual-clutch transmission fluid changes will relentlessly destroy the internal wet clutch pack and the highly sensitive Mechatronic control unit. A full transmission rebuild easily costs upwards of 3,000 EUR.",
      "Drivability Loss (DPF/EGR Choking): Severe carbon and ash buildup from city driving will eventually restrict exhaust and intake airflow so heavily that the engine physically cannot breathe at higher RPMs. The engine will suffer from random misfires and incredibly poor acceleration.",
      "Fuel System Destruction (HPFP Failure): Accidentally filling the car with petrol, or running the fuel tank completely dry, will instantly score the internals of the high-pressure fuel pump due to a lack of diesel lubricity. This sends fine metal shavings directly into the delicate solenoid injectors, utterly destroying the entire high-pressure fuel system."
    ],
