    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine. The M 266 utilizes a simplex timing chain. While catastrophic stretching is relatively rare compared to modern Mercedes engines, the hydraulic tensioner can fail at high mileages, causing a distinct cold-start rattle. The true nightmare is the packaging: because the engine is tilted forward at an extreme 59-degree angle and tucked under the firewall, accessing the timing components or the turbocharger requires physically unbolting and dropping the entire engine and front subframe assembly. This turns preventative maintenance into an astronomical labor job.)",
    injectionType: "Multi-Point Port Injection (Features a traditional Bosch port injection system. Because the fuel washes directly over the intake valves, this engine is completely immune to the severe carbon sludge buildup issues that plague modern direct-injected turbo engines. The system is extremely robust, requiring no Walnut Blasting. Fuel filter and injector maintenance are standard.)",
    engineLifespan: "250,000 - 300,000+ km (The mechanical core is an exceptionally robust cast-iron block with an aluminum cylinder head. However, the addition of a turbocharger significantly increases thermal stress on the engine bay compared to the naturally aspirated models. The vehicle's actual economic lifespan is dictated entirely by its 'sandwich' packaging. When the turbocharger, starter motor, or alternator fails, the massive engine-out labor costs frequently total the vehicle economically.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Intercooled. Equipped with a BorgWarner/KKK turbocharger. It provides excellent low-end torque and transforms the heavy B-Class into a genuine sleeper. However, it is buried deep against the firewall. The extreme heat bakes surrounding hoses, and replacing a failed turbo requires dropping the entire engine and subframe.)",
    ecuType: "Siemens SIM266 (A highly robust engine management system. However, the ECU is physically mounted directly on top of the engine airbox. This location subjects the delicate electronics to intense heat cycling and severe vibrations. Over time, this causes the internal solder joints to fracture, leading to mysterious engine stalls, misfires, or non-start conditions. It is receptive to OBD2 flashing for significant power gains.)",
