    timingType: "Chain (Located at the REAR of the engine block, leaning towards the firewall. The OM 640 utilizes a simplex timing chain system. Because the engine is tilted forward at an extreme 59-degree angle in the sandwich floor, accessing the timing components requires dropping the entire engine and front subframe out of the vehicle. While catastrophic chain snapping is rare if oil is maintained, tensioner degradation causes cold-start rattles. Any timing service is an astronomical labor job.)",
    injectionType: "Common Rail Direct Injection (Features a reliable Bosch common rail system. While the injectors themselves are robust, the copper seals at the base of the injectors are notorious for failing over time. This allows combustion gases to escape, creating a thick, tar-like carbon buildup known as 'Black Death' around the injectors. If not caught early, removing the cemented injectors is incredibly difficult and risks destroying the cylinder head.)",
    engineLifespan: "400,000+ km (The mechanical core of the OM 640 is a phenomenally strong cast-iron block. Because it is a 2.0L engine severely detuned to only 109 horsepower, the internal rotating assembly is virtually unstressed and will easily outlive the chassis. However, the vehicle's economic lifespan is entirely dictated by its disastrous 'sandwich' packaging. When auxiliary components like the starter motor fail, the massive engine-out labor costs frequently total the vehicle economically.)",
    turboType: "Turbocharged (Equipped with a Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT) turbocharger. It provides excellent low-end torque. The turbo is highly reliable, but the vacuum actuator controlling the variable vanes can stick due to heavy carbon buildup from low-speed urban driving. This causes the turbo to fail to build boost, resulting in Limp Mode. Replacing the turbo requires an engine drop.)",
    ecuType: "Bosch EDC16C31 (A highly robust diesel engine management system. It meticulously controls the common rail pressure, the VNT actuator, and the EGR cycle. The ECU is mounted in the engine bay, subjecting it to intense heat. It is incredibly receptive to OBD2 flashing, allowing tuners to easily unlock hidden factory power reserves.)",
