    drivingExperience: "The driving experience of the 120-horsepower B 200 CDI Fleet Edition feels deliberately restricted. The 2.0-liter OM640 block delivers 280 Nm of torque almost immediately, pulling the heavy W245 chassis with remarkable ease off the line. However, the artificial software restriction imposed by Mercedes to bypass corporate taxes or fleet insurance brackets becomes glaringly obvious on the highway; the engine simply stops pulling past 3,500 RPM. The engine is undeniably loud and agricultural, with significant diesel clatter penetrating the cabin at idle. The unique 'sandwich floor' architecture means you sit unusually high, providing excellent visibility that mimics a small SUV. If paired with the Autotronic CVT transmission, acceleration is smooth without gear steps, but it exhibits a rubber-band feeling. The 6-speed manual is far more engaging. A Stage 1 flash transforms this car into a truly punchy commuter, completely overriding the fleet limits.",
    mechanicVerdict: "The OM 640.941 found in the W245 B 200 CDI Fleet Edition is a robust 2.0-liter turbodiesel that is severely compromised by its disastrous packaging, earning an 8/10 risk rating. Because this specific variant is artificially software-restricted to 120 horsepower to appease fleet managers, the engine is completely unstressed and responds beautifully to a Stage 1 tune. Mercedes-Benz designed this vehicle around the innovative sandwich floor concept, meaning the engine is tilted forward at an extreme 59-degree angle. While the core cast-iron block and common rail injection system are incredibly durable and can easily exceed 400,000 kilometers, the serviceability is a complete nightmare. Replacing simple auxiliary components like the starter motor or alternator requires a mechanic to unbolt and lower the entire engine and front subframe out of the vehicle. This turns basic preventative maintenance into an astronomical, multi-day labor bill that frequently exceeds the vehicle's market value. The OM 640 utilizes a reliable timing chain, but if the tensioner fails or the chain stretches, the rearward placement dictates another massive engine-out job. The fuel injectors are prone to seal leakage over time, leading to the infamous 'Black Death' carbon buildup if not caught early. The Exhaust Gas Recirculation valve and intake manifold frequently clog with sticky soot due to slow urban driving. If equipped with the Autotronic 722.8 CVT, the transmission becomes a massive financial liability due to constantly failing internal speed sensors and fragility under diesel torque. The front coil springs also snap frequently under the heavy weight of the diesel powertrain and sandwich floor architecture. Ultimately, while the B 200 CDI Fleet Edition is a highly practical family car, the terrifying engine-out labor costs make it a risky second-hand purchase.",
    repairCostEstimate: "$1,500 - $3,000+ / year (The labor is astronomical. If the starter motor or alternator fails, budget 1,200+ EUR purely because the engine subframe must be dropped. If the Autotronic CVT speed sensors fail, valve body repair is 800-1,200 EUR. A snapped front coil spring replacement is 250 EUR. Fixing 'Black Death' injector leaks can exceed 500 EUR if they are seized.)"
